1
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:03,580
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,280
Welcome back to another great lesson here at EnglishPod.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:07,680
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:08,980
And my name is Catherine.

5
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:15,020
So, Marco, today we've got a little bit of an unusual lesson because we're talking about hypothetical situations.

6
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:15,620
Right.

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,180
We're talking about a unreal situation or something that may happen or may not happen.

8
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:21,840
Okay.

9
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:24,480
So, you're going to hear all about this in today's dialogue.

10
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:25,680
We're going to hear it a couple of times.

11
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:28,480
When we come back, we'll be breaking it down for you.

12
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:34,020
So, you know all about these great phrases and how to talk about not real situations with your friends.

13
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:40,400
Okay.

14
00:00:42,570 --> 00:00:43,110
Next question.

15
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,480
If Eric asked you out on a date, what would you say?

16
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:46,810
Duh.

17
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:47,830
I would say yes.

18
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,190
Eric is the most popular kid in school.

19
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:51,270
Okay.

20
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:51,770
My turn.

21
00:00:52,630 --> 00:00:54,790
What would you do if you won the lottery?

22
00:00:55,490 --> 00:00:56,270
Let's see.

23
00:00:57,030 --> 00:01:01,150
If I won the lottery, I would buy two tickets for trips around the world.

24
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:04,730
If you buy me a ticket, I'll go with you for sure.

25
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:09,350
My dad would freak out if I even mention a trip like that.

26
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:10,680
All right.

27
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:11,030
This is a good one.

28
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,690
What would your mom say if you told her that you're going to get married?

29
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:17,990
If I told her that, she would faint and have me committed.

30
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:23,240
All right.

31
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:34,760
So, I think that was clear and well, a very common situation two teenage girls talking to each other about, you know, boys and what would you do and da-da-da.

32
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:35,410
Guys do this too.

33
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:37,470
So, don't just put this on the girls, okay?

34
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:39,270
I don't think we do it like this though.

35
00:01:39,450 --> 00:01:42,710
No, but it's actually a very popular game in the car to play.

36
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:43,590
The what if game.

37
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:45,040
The what if game.

38
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,040
Yeah.

39
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:47,040
Exactly.

40
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:51,240
And that's exactly the topic that we want to talk about today is the structure what if which is actually called a conditional.

41
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:52,240
All right.

42
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:58,160
It's called a conditional because the results, the thing that happens depends on something else.

43
00:01:58,160 --> 00:01:59,160
Exactly.

44
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:03,610
So, let's actually take a look at the words in today's language takeaway before we talk about all this grammar stuff.

45
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:06,440
Language takeaway.

46
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:08,040
Okay.

47
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:10,480
So, we'll start out with some basic words.

48
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:15,980
Well, you know, they were talking about guys and she said if Eric asked you out.

49
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:17,500
Right?

50
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:20,080
So, to ask somebody out.

51
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:24,140
Okay, so this might be a little bit strange because you think, okay, to ask is to ask a question.

52
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:27,320
But to ask out, it's a fixed phrase.

53
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:29,800
It's something you say, these two words go together, right?

54
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:35,380
So, I want to ask someone out, that means I want to invite someone on a date.

55
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:37,920
On a romantic date, usually.

56
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:38,360
So, yeah.

57
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:42,200
I want to ask him out to dinner or he asked me out to the movies.

58
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:47,980
Now, if I didn't want to ask somebody out on a romantic date, what verb would you use?

59
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:50,220
I'd say you could invite someone out.

60
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:57,620
But then again, it's always a little bit, well, you can say invite for someone if you're going to ask them on a date too.

61
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:03,480
So, I'd say to ask out is always romantic and to invite can be romantic or just friends.

62
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:04,320
Okay.

63
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,080
So, to ask somebody out.

64
00:03:06,460 --> 00:03:14,200
Now, they were talking about other situations, maybe they would go on a trip or something and she said my dad would freak out.

65
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:18,980
This is a great phrase, this is something that I used a lot when I was a teenager.

66
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:20,100
The word freak out?

67
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:21,600
Freak out.

68
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:29,000
So, the verb to freak out means to be really angry or upset or, you know, it could be a good thing or a bad thing.

69
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:31,900
But usually, when we're talking about our parents, it's a bad thing.

70
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:33,080
It's a bad thing.

71
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,540
So, they get angry, they start yelling at you.

72
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:35,140
Yeah.

73
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:37,840
So, freak out means like overreact.

74
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:38,840
All right.

75
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:43,480
The last word that we have in language takeaway today is committed.

76
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:45,690
Well let's look at the whole phrase.

77
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:48,040
He says she would have me committed.

78
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:50,990
So, this is actually being done to someone.

79
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:56,030
Now, we did look at this word before but basically for marriage, right?

80
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:58,480
To commit to somebody, to promise.

81
00:03:58,850 --> 00:04:06,890
Yeah, but to have someone committed means that you're going to send them away to maybe a mental institution.

82
00:04:07,010 --> 00:04:07,250
Exactly.

83
00:04:07,830 --> 00:04:14,710
So, this means they have psychological problems and someone else sends them to the hospital because they can't send themselves.

84
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:15,550
Exactly.

85
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:19,630
So, it's always used to have someone committed, right?

86
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,770
Right, because it's being done to them.

87
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:23,270
They don't want it to happen.

88
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:23,910
It's involuntary.

89
00:04:24,930 --> 00:04:25,230
Okay.

90
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:28,520
So, she had him committed.

91
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:32,340
My mom's going to have me committed if I keep working 20 hours a day.

92
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:33,240
Exactly.

93
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:34,800
So, that's the way you use it.

94
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,600
So, now you have another way of using this verb.

95
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:40,180
But now I think we should listen to this dialogue again.

96
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:45,740
We're going to slow it down a little bit and then we'll come back and look at this grammar focus that we want to talk about.

97
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:53,480
Okay, next question.

98
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:56,570
If Eric asked you out on a date, what would you say?

99
00:04:57,320 --> 00:04:59,220
Duh, I would say yes.

100
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:02,520
Eric is the most popular kid in school.

101
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:04,800
Okay, my turn.

102
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:08,340
What would you do if you won the lottery?

103
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:10,180
Let's see.

104
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:16,360
If I won the lottery, I would buy two tickets for a trip around the world.

105
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:20,600
If you buy me a ticket, I will go with you for sure.

106
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:26,200
My dad would freak out if I even mention a trip like that.

107
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:28,780
Alright, this is a good one.

108
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:33,740
What would your mom say if you told her you were going to get married?

109
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:37,920
If I told her that, she would faint and have me committed.

110
00:05:42,310 --> 00:05:45,130
Alright, so we understood all the vocab now.

111
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:49,810
Now let's take a look at these unreal situations and what we were saying, the what if game.

112
00:05:50,270 --> 00:05:55,230
Right, so I think it's important to push these into two different categories, right?

113
00:05:55,370 --> 00:06:01,290
We have one kind where it's very unrealistic, it's probably not going to happen, that kind of situation.

114
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:08,270
And the other one is a situation that could very well happen, something that's probably very common, okay?

115
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:13,590
So the first one we're going to talk about is very unrealistic situations, things that probably won't happen.

116
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:17,630
Okay, so let's take a look at an example that we found in the dialogue.

117
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:24,800
The most common one and I think everybody gets asked this question is, if you won the lottery, what would you do?

118
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:26,840
Okay, if you won the lottery.

119
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,880
So this is a situation that's very uncommon, right?

120
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:31,260
Not many people win the lottery.

121
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:38,080
So we have to say, if you won the lottery, so we use this verb in the past tense.

122
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:41,880
If you won the lottery, if this happened, what would you do?

123
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:42,340
Right.

124
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:45,210
So, Catherine, if you won the lottery, what would you do?

125
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:50,950
If I won the lottery, I would buy a boat and sail around the world with my friends.

126
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:55,800
Alright, so another example, again, using the same structure, right?

127
00:06:55,800 --> 00:07:04,040
The verb in the simple past tense, we would say, if you were stuck on an island with one other person, who would that be?

128
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:11,600
Alright, so if you were stuck on a desert island with one person, who would that person be?

129
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:12,800
So Marco, I'm going to ask you, who would it be?

130
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:18,260
I think it would be an engineer or a scientist that could probably get me off the island.

131
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:19,400
What about your girlfriend?

132
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:23,680
No, no, well, you know, that's the point, if I can get to her quicker.

133
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:23,860
Okay.

134
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,720
I would say maybe kind of romantic, but kind of not.

135
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:29,720
So that's the first situation.

136
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:33,020
We've got these situations like being stuck on an island, winning the lottery.

137
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:35,360
These are very unlikely.

138
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:36,380
But what about the other kind?

139
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:47,120
But before we move on to that, you notice that we use the verb was, we didn't say, if I was on an island, I said, if I were, right?

140
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:48,120
Or if you were.

141
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:50,880
So this is the way that you use it in these conditionals.

142
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:55,960
If I were you, or if you were president of the United States.

143
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:57,360
Yeah, this is a rule.

144
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:58,210
So this is a grammar rule.

145
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:01,850
We're not going to describe why it happens because it's very, very complicated.

146
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:03,050
And you can look it up on our site.

147
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,510
But we're going to describe how to use it.

148
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:06,310
That's the most important.

149
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:12,250
So we can say, for example, Marco, if I were you, I would dye my hair red.

150
00:08:12,890 --> 00:08:13,250
Why?

151
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:13,910
I don't know.

152
00:08:13,950 --> 00:08:14,630
I think it would look cool.

153
00:08:14,930 --> 00:08:15,970
I don't think so.

154
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:18,570
So if I were you, I would.

155
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,320
Or if you were me, what would you do?

156
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:22,320
Exactly.

157
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,680
So we always use it like this, right?

158
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:28,600
If you're going to use this conditional, always use were for the subject.

159
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:29,560
Never was.

160
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:30,240
Right.

161
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:30,960
So you can tell.

162
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:33,200
This is a very, very big grammar issue.

163
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:36,040
A lot of English speakers make this mistake.

164
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:36,800
Yes, very many.

165
00:08:37,380 --> 00:08:41,160
But now you know that you shouldn't make this mistake and you can speak properly.

166
00:08:41,610 --> 00:08:50,570
All right, so now that we've seen these semi-unreal situations, let's move on to something that's a little bit more probable, that it may happen.

167
00:08:51,110 --> 00:08:51,290
Right.

168
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:55,330
So we've got these situations that are common or likely.

169
00:08:56,230 --> 00:09:00,250
For example, in this lesson we hear, I will go with you for sure.

170
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:04,390
So this is, if you buy me a ticket to travel, of course I will go with you.

171
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:10,600
So if you buy, this is in the present tense, if you buy, then I will.

172
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:10,830
Right.

173
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:12,600
Right.

174
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:13,600
So now you notice the difference.

175
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:17,190
The verb is in the present and then we use, instead of would, we use will.

176
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:24,850
So again, I can say, if it rains tomorrow, I will stay home and watch TV all day.

177
00:09:25,270 --> 00:09:26,990
Okay, so think about this.

178
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:31,770
Before we talked about going to desert islands, now we're talking about rain and the weather.

179
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:33,390
They're very, very different.

180
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:35,330
The rain is, you know, it's very common.

181
00:09:35,590 --> 00:09:39,410
So we can say, if it rains tomorrow, I will need an umbrella.

182
00:09:40,050 --> 00:09:40,690
What about this?

183
00:09:41,150 --> 00:09:42,790
If we have lunch tomorrow, what will we eat?

184
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:46,130
Okay, so we have lunch pretty much every day, right?

185
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:47,110
Yeah, pretty much.

186
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:50,410
So again, it's a situation that is very probable.

187
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:57,110
So if we have lunch tomorrow, we will probably eat, I don't know, pasta, I think.

188
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:58,280
I'm feeling like pasta.

189
00:09:58,280 --> 00:09:59,150
Pasta, noodles.

190
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:00,280
Yeah.

191
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:10,400
All right, so Marco, let's take another listen to some of these phrases and try and figure out while you're listening, which kind of conditional it is, first or the second.

192
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:11,400
Example one.

193
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:16,880
If it's sunny today, we will go to the park.

194
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:17,880
Example two.

195
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:22,320
I would get a divorce if I were you.

196
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:23,320
Example three.

197
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:27,000
Mary will be very sad if Joe leaves.

198
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,160
Example four.

199
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:34,560
We wouldn't be so late if Nick drove faster.

200
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:37,130
Very good.

201
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:46,570
So we heard different examples and the first conditional is the one that we mentioned is very probable and the second conditional is the one that's not so probable.

202
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:48,370
So they're very easy to distinguish.

203
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:52,760
You don't really need to know the names, just know how to use them, right?

204
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:53,760
Exactly.

205
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:57,600
And so just remember, if you were me, what would you do?

206
00:10:57,600 --> 00:11:00,380
We've got these kind of pairs, these words that go together.

207
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:02,720
And the other one is more common.

208
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:05,440
If it rains tomorrow, I will bring an umbrella.

209
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:06,640
Perfect.

210
00:11:07,020 --> 00:11:14,080
So why don't we listen to this dialogue for the last time just to kind of reinforce everything and then we'll come back and talk a little bit more.

211
00:11:21,290 --> 00:11:22,410
Okay next question.

212
00:11:23,030 --> 00:11:25,070
If Eric asked you out on a date, what would you say?

213
00:11:25,750 --> 00:11:27,130
Duh, I would say yes.

214
00:11:27,790 --> 00:11:29,490
Eric is the most popular kid in school.

215
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:31,080
Okay, my turn.

216
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:34,080
What would you do if you won the lottery?

217
00:11:34,900 --> 00:11:35,560
Let's see.

218
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:40,460
If I won the lottery, I would buy two tickets for trips around the world.

219
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:44,060
If you buy me a ticket, I'll go with you for sure.

220
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:48,660
My dad would freak out if I even mention a trip like that.

221
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:51,100
All right, this is a good one.

222
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:53,940
What would your mom say if you told her that you're going to get married?

223
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:57,320
If I told her that, she would faint and have me committed.

224
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:10,120
So Marco, if we talk any more about grammar, someone's going to have to have me committed.

225
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:16,930
It is a little bit difficult to talk about grammar, especially on a podcast because it's such a difficult topic and it's also kind of heavy, right?

226
00:12:17,350 --> 00:12:18,570
It is, but I'll tell you this.

227
00:12:19,050 --> 00:12:21,070
These are very, very common phrases.

228
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:24,080
People use these all the time.

229
00:12:24,080 --> 00:12:25,510
We talk in hypotheticals very often.

230
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:34,690
So if you're asking for advice, for example, or if you're just playing a game in the car, you're going to encounter these and it's very important to be able to know how to use them.

